Eu(III)-Functionalized In-MOF (In(OH)bpydc) as Fluorescent Probe for Highly Selectively Sensing Organic Small Molecules and Anions
Especially for CHCl3 and $\mathbf{MnO}_{4}^{-}$
School of Chemical Science and Engineering, Tongji University, Shanghai 200092, China
Abstract
A highly fluorescent metal–organic framework (In-MOF, In(OH)bpydc (bpydc $\mathbf{\tau}=\mathbf{\tau}$ biphenyl-5,5’- dicarboxylate)) imparting additional luminescent ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions has been synthesized for sensing organic molecules and anions, especially chloroform and $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ , both of them have a significant spectral response. This $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ -incorporated hybrid (In-MOF-Eu) has been fully characterized, which reveals large surface area, great pore size, favorable thermal stability, excellent luminescence as well as good fluorescence stability. On the one hand, the dual-emitting MOF hybrid exhibits a distinctly different response to the chloroform, one of which is almost unchanged, while the other intrinsic broad emission has a remarkable enhancement, leading to an obvious change in color; on the other hand, it also shows a high selectivity for detection of $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ because of the largest quenching effect on the luminescent emission, which results in a dark emission. This work represents a new approach for the fabrication of highly selective and sensitive probe for detection of chloroform and $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ , as it underlines the opportunity of MOFs with prominent optical properties to explore the existing target substance in the environment.
Keywords: In-based metal-organic frameworks; lanthanide ion; sensor; fluorescence probe, chloroform; $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ .
## 1. Introduction
In recent years, the up-to-date emerging of MOF materials has attracted the attention of many people for they have good performance, which can be applied to various aspects, such as gas adsorption [1,2], catalysis [3-5], sensor [6,7] and device assembly [8,9]. As one classic of the family members in MOFs, Ln-MOFs, which contain rare earth ions in the pore, have more superior properties, because they not only have characteristic of the MOF itself, but also combine the luminescent properties of rare earth elements, greatly expanding the application values [10-13]. It should be highlighted that the reasons why the Ln-MOF materials have the great luminescent application value are the pure color, the excellent optical stability, the long luminescent lifetime and the high luminescence quantum efficiencies [14-16]. Especially in sensing, there are a great deal of researches on all kinds of species such as iron ions [17,18], amines [19,20] and ketones [21,22], which are classic, but common and still limited. To presence, the numbers of literatures are increasing on new Ln-MOFs for sensing, for example, MOF-253 [23] has significant optical response to $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ ,as well as MIL-121 [24] has great selectivity for ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ . At present, several relevant mechanisms have been put forward to explore the coordination between metal and ligands, as well as the energy transfer between the ligands and the molecules [25-27]. Special note is that the postsynthetic method (PSM) is an extremely popular strategy for the composition of lanthanide-based MOFs, which can be directly performed on the material rather than molecule precursors [28-31].
Unfortunately, the reports about chloroform and permanganate ions detection are still relatively infrequent. As we all know, both of them are toxic in aqueous solutions, so they are typically used as disinfectants. Chlorine used to be employed to disinfect drinking water, which will produce the byproduct, chloroform. Chloroform is toxic organic compound, which has anesthetic effect on nerves and leads to the deadly damage on central nervous system, heart, liver and kidney [32-34]. In addition, chloroform will form phosgene under ultraviolet or high heat [35-37]. This gas is not merely highly toxic, but also one of the environmental pollutants. As for the permanganate ion $\left(\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-\cdot}\right)$ ), a strong oxidizing agent, utilized for a variety of purposes in analytical chemistry, agriculture, municipal water treatment, human health and safety, medicine, groundwater remediation and aquaculture. Potassium permanganate is used in the fish production stage of pond aquaculture to treat a variety of fish diseases; however, potassium permanganate aqueous solution has not been authoritatively recognized as a desired aquaculture therapeutant, because it has side effects when the concentration is too high, which will give rise to the aquatic ecological risk, only potassium permanganate solution with low concentration has positive applications for disinfecting [38-40]. As the literature reported [41, 42], median lethal concentration of $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ to pimephales promelas (a kind of river fish) is $0.08\ \mathrm{mmol/L}$ , while to flea (a kind of insect), the value is as low as 0.02 mmol/L; ingestion of $\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ solutions causes gastrointestinal distress, while bolus ingestion has caused respiratory arrest following coagulative necrosis and hemorrhage in the esophagus, stomach, or liver. So a Do Not Consume concentration of $7~\mathrm{\mg/L~\KMnO}_{4}$ is recommended. Therefore, the detection for the content of chloroform or permanganate anions in water has a pretty good practical significance. At present, there are no more than a small number of fluorescent sensors for these two substances, such as Ba-O-LaMOF [43], Ni-MOF [44], and Mg-MOF [45], so the study of selective probes is still a challenge now.
In this context, In-MOF (In(OH)bpydc) is chosen as a parent framework for encapsulating lanthanide, which is a simple and convenient strategy for selectively fluorescent sensing about chloroform and potassium dichromate (Scheme 1). Further, $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ as the center metal ions, $^{2,2^{\circ}}$ -bipyridine-5,5’- dicarboxylic acid $\mathbf{\bar{H}}_{2}$ bpydc) as the organic linker and then lanthanide as activators, all of them are used to construct a luminescent lanthanide metal-organic frameworks (named In-MOF-Ln) via hydrothermal method [46], which has been proved successful in our previous work [47]. In addition, $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ ion is chosen as the activator for its favorable luminescence performance in all kinds of functionalized MOFs systems, such as bio-MOF-1 [48-50], Ag-Ln MOFs [51], and Eu-MOF [16]. Thanks to the $\mathrm{{}^{5}D_{0}}$ - $\mathrm{{}^{7}F_{\mathrm{{J}}}(J=0{-}4)}$ transitions of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ , the materials will produce a clear pure red emission and have long luminescence lifetimes as well as good luminescence efficiency [52,53]. Features of this material have been fully characterized via a variety of methods, which prove that the structure and luminescence of the material are quite stable in water or organic solvents. The lanthanide-doped luminescence MOFs is a robust platform for the fluorescent detection that displays obvious change in emission color. What's more, the experimental results indicate that the In-MOF-Eu has excellent selectivity to chloroform and permanganate anions, which greatly confirm it can be serve as an effective probe to detect these substances. The relative fluorescent intensity of In-MOF-Eu significantly enhance as soon as it encounters chloroform, while the emission peaks of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ almost no change; but for other organic solvents, the spectrum will not changes so obviously, the results in detail will be discussed in the post text. As for the anions, spectral emissions of permanganate anions will become bleak. This apparent quenching effect can discriminate it from a large number of other anions.  Further experiments reveal that the additional anions do not result in a disturbance to the quenching effect.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Chemicals
$\mathrm{Eu(NO_{3})_{3}}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was obtained by oxidation of $\mathrm{Eu}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ dissolved in hot concentrated nitric acid. All of the other chemicals were of reagent grade and obtained commercially without further purification. Both of $\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (408.93 g·mol-1) and the organic ligand $^{2,2^{\circ}}$ -bipyridine- $_{5,5},$ -dicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{\bf~\leftmoon}(\mathrm{\bf~H}_{2}$ bpydc, $244.20\ \mathrm{g{\cdot}m o l^{-1}}$ were purchased from Aldrich to synthesize In(OH)bpydc (In-MOF). All aqueous solutions of F-, Cl-, Br-, I-, $\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}$ , ${\mathrm{SO}}_{4}^{2\cdot}$ -, $\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2\cdot}$ -, $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}$ were prepared from sodium or potassium salts.
## 2.2. Instrumentation
## 2.3. Synthesis of In(OH)(bpydc) (In-MOF)
In a feasible synthesis of In-MOF, In(NO ) ·6H O (409.0 mg, $1.0\ \mathrm{mmol}\$ ) and $\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\cdot}$ bpydc $(244.2~\mathrm{mg}$ , 1.0 mmol) were dissolved in DMF ( $20~\mathrm{mL},$ at room temperature, which was further stirred for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ in a $40~\mathrm{mL}$ solvothermal vessel, and was subsequently heated to $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ . In order to remove any organic matter encapsulated within the pores of the framework, the products were washed by Soxhlet extraction in methanol for 24 h and then dried at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum overnight. Subsequently, the resulting product was then collected by centrifugation, before being washed for several times with DMF and dried in vacuum environment. We denoted the synthesized In(OH)(bpydc) as In-MOF.
## 2.4. Synthesis of In-MOF-Eu
In-MOF-Eu was prepared according to the postsynthetic modification method. The mixture of 186.5 $\mathrm{mg}\left(0.5\mathrm{mmol}\right)$ of In-MOF and $114\mathrm{mg}$ (0.25 mmol) $\mathrm{Eu(NO_{3})_{3}}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in $25~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF are stirred at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ . The resulting white solid was then separated from the mixed dispersion by centrifugation, and washed with methanol in order to remove redundant ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ cations on the surface of the frameworks. Finally, the resulted white powder was dried under vacuum at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . The white synthesized products were denoted as In-MOF-Eu.
## 2.5. Detection of anions in aqueous solution
We carried out the fluorescent detection of anions at room temperature in de-ionized water. The corresponding metal salts were dissolved into DI-water, and prepared into a concentration of 0.5 mmol/L aqueous solutions (F-, Cl-, Br-, I-, $\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2\cdot}$ -, $\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2\cdot}$ -, $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-})$ , respectively. Subsequently, $3\mathrm{mg}$ of In-MOF-Eu and $3\mathrm{mL}$ of $0.5\mathrm{mmol/L}$ aqueous solutions of corresponding anions were added into a $4\mathrm{mL}$ centrifuge tube, and then the mixture was equilibrated thoroughly for 5 min by ultrasound processing. After the luminescent experiment, 1 mL $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ (0.5 mmol/L) solution was poured into these solutions containing respective metal ions in order to further explore the interference experiment of $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ .
## 2.6. Detection of small organic molecules
For sensing small organic molecules, In-MOF-Eu powders $(3~\mathrm{mg})$ were added into various organic solvents $(3\ \mathrm{mL})$ at room temperature such as chloroform, methanol, ethanol, ethylene glycol, 1,2- dichloroethane, acetone, pyridine, N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) and tetrahydrofuran (THF). Ultimately, the mixtures were then sonicated adequately for $5~\mathrm{min}$ to prepare a suspension, which incorporates In-MOF-Eu for the next luminescence measurements.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of In-MOF and In-MOF-Eu
To demonstrate our strategy, the In-MOF is selected and synthesized with a mixture of H bpydc and $\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in DMF by solvothermal method. In-MOF is chosen for several reasons: (i) good thermal and chemical stability in the air; (ii) intense ligand-central emission; (iii) enough pores and Lewis basic sites of bipyridyl moieties for incorporating and sensitizing lanthanide cations. As depicted in previous reports, the structure of In-MOF is built up from an infinite chain of InO6 corner-sharing octahedra, which are interconnected by bpydc2- ligands to construct rhombic shaped pores [45]. The molecular structure model of In-MOF is given and the red octahedra present the octahedral InO6 (Fig.
S1, supporting information). Based on this, we further synthesize the luminescent ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ loaded In-MOF via postsynthetic method (PSM). Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of the as-synthesized InMOF and In-MOF-Eu have been measured in Fig. S2 (Supporting information), both of which are in good agreement with that of literature [45] and suggest that the structure still remains unchanged after the incorporation of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ . The morphology of In-MOF and In-MOF-Eu is studied by SEM, revealing the particles with crystal sizes in the range of $10{-}30~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ (Fig. S2b, S2c). Besides, the amount of $\mathbf{In}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ cations loaded in In-MOF has been determined by ICPMS, with the atomic $\%$ of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}/\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ of $22.6\%$ .
The FTIR spectra of the In-MOF-Eu are similar to that of In-MOF (Fig. S3, ESI). The peak at $1350\mathrm{cm}^{\cdot}$ 1 is ascribed to the C-N stretching of the bipyridine. The absorption at $1670~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ is assigned to the asymmetric stretching vibrations of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C}}={\mathrm{O}}$ , which explains the existences of carboxylic group. The absorption band located at 1050 and $780~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be assigned to the C–H deformation vibrations of the aromatic ring. Thermogravimetric analysis (Fig. S4, supporting information) shows two main weight losses in the curves. The first weight loss occurs at the temperature range of ${\sim}200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ can be ascribed to the decomposition of solvents in pores. The second weight loss is in the temperature range of 400– $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , assigned to the destruction of the framework. Loading level of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ cations is determined by ICP and shown in Table S1 (supporting information), and the ratio of $\mathrm{In}^{3+}/\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ is 1:0.188.
## 3.2. Luminescence properties of In-MOF and In-MOF-Eu
The luminescence of the solid In-MOF and In-MOF-Eu is investigated at room temperature. As shown in Fig. S5a (supporting information), the excitation spectra of In-MOF is obtained by monitoring the emission at $480\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which exhibits a band located at about $390\mathrm{nm}$ . The intense broad band of ligandcentral emission is located at 420 to $700~\mathrm{nm}$ , which comprehensively resulted in light with blue-green color, as depicted in the pink curve of luminescent spectra. That phenomenon can be accounted for by ligand-to-metal charge transfer. The blue-green color of In-MOF has also been proved vividly by the CIE chromaticity diagram $(\mathrm{X}=0.24,\mathrm{Y}=0.35)$ and the inserted optical photo obtained at $_\mathrm{ex}=390\:\mathrm{nm}$ via using a Xe lamp as the excitation source (Fig. S5b, supporting information). Generally speaking, the ligands greatly affect the excitation wavelength of a trivalent lanthanide ion, which is the so called “antenna effect” [54]. The ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ characteristic transitions at 580, 595, 616, 650 and $698\ \mathrm{nm}$ under excitation at $305~\mathrm{nm}$ can be easily observed in the emission spectrum of In-MOF-Eu due to the $\mathrm{^5\widehat{D}_{0}-^{7}F_{\mathrm{_J}}}$ ( ${\mathrm{~J~}=0{-}4})$ transitions (Fig. 1a). It is noteworthy that the broad band at $550~\mathrm{nm}$ is the ligand-central emission. The lanthanides of In-MOF-Eu can be sensitized efficiently by energy transfer according to the excitation spectrum. The CIE diagram (Fig. 1a) and the inserted photo of the In-MOF-Eu show that the compound emits a near red light under UV-light when it is excited at 305 nm. Detection of the luminescent spectrum of In-MOF-Eu at λ $_\mathrm{ex}=360\ \mathrm{nm}$ has also been conducted, as shown in Fig 1a-b, where the band of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ decreases while the ligand-central one increases. For the study of stability of InMOF-Eu, the compound is immersed into the aqueous solution for several days whose result has been displayed in the Fig. S6 (supporting information), in which the day-to-day curves indicate that the fluorescence of In-MOF-Eu remains almost same during 9 days. The detailed relative intensity at $_\mathrm{em}=$ $615\ \mathrm{nm}$ exhibited in the inserted graph of Fig. S6 (supporting information), gives us a clear proof proving the faint change of In-MOF-Eu and telling us indirectly that the structure of the hybrid remains well.
## 3.3. Detection of small molecules
To In light of the excellent luminescence and good water stability of In-MOF-Eu, we examine the potential application of In-MOF-Eu for detecting small molecules. The as-synthesized sample $\mathrm{3\mg}\mathrm{\dot{\Omega}}$ is simply immersed in the organic ( $3~\mathrm{mL}$ ) in sequence. The luminescent responses of In-MOF-Eu toward small molecules of various organics are recorded in Fig. 2 at λ $_{\mathrm{ex}}=360~\mathrm{nm}$ . The objects to verify are those frequently used organics include chloroform, methanol, ethanol, ethylene glycol, 1,2- dichloroethane, acetone, pyridine, N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) and tetrahydrofuran (THF). Besides, deionized water is chosen as a reference for comparison. The results indicate that various organics display greatly different effects on the luminescence of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions.
emission of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ and ligand-central emission. In the first part, compared to the ligand-central emission, the relative intensities of sharp lines centered at $615\mathrm{nm}$ of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ have little difference among each other. But the second part gives us an interesting comparison, in which the In-MOF-Eu displays a highest emission located in around $490\ \mathrm{nm}$ and significantly higher than the rest. As shown in the inset of Fig. 2, these dark colors of organics serve as foils to that of chloroform, leading to the notable distinction by our naked eyes. After that, the power PXRD for all these organics is similar to that of In-MOF-Eu, suggesting that the basic frameworks remain unchanged (Fig. S7, supporting information). The CIE chromaticity diagram of In-MOF-Eu and coordinate of CIE chromaticity diagram after immersing in different organic solvents have been given in Fig. S8 (supporting information) and Table. S2 (supporting information), respectively. Almost the points at the CIE chromaticity diagram are concentrated on the blue region, of which the point of chloroform is located on the far left, also revealing that the ligand-central emission play a dominated role in the luminescence. On the basis of Table 1, photophysical data of the intensity ratios of I $(490\mathrm{nm}$ ) / I (616 nm) show that ratio of $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ is the maximum value. The longest corresponding lifetime of In-MOF-Eu in $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ is $137.43~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ and much higher than those of the rest. Guided by the above discussion, In-MOF-Eu shows highly selective for chloroform. As shown in Fig. S9 (supporting information), we also compare the luminescence intensity of In-MOF-Eu among several similar halogenated compounds, which asserts that luminescence intensity of ligand-central emission in $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ is still the highest, differing from the rest. In-MOF-Eu powder is immersed in Ethanol as the standard suspension, and the $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ content is successively increased to detect the luminescent response. Fig. 3a shows the emission spectra of InMOF-Eu dispersed in $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ –ethanol mixed solvent with different volume ratio $(\mathrm{\DeltaV_{cHCl3}/V_{\mathrm{\mathrm{ethanol}}}})$ . The luminescence intensity of In-MOF-Eu suspension enhances progressively with the increase of the proportion of $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ . In order to conduct an inquiry into the time-response characteristic of the InMOF-Eu sensor toward chloroform, we measure the fluorescence intensity of In-MOF-Eu as a function of immersion time in chloroform, as depicted in the time-response PL spectra and curves within $35\mathrm{min}$ of Fig. 3b. Just for 5 min, the luminescence intensity at $490\mathrm{nm}$ of In-MOF-Eu reached a constant value and after that, the height is raised just a little. As time changes, the PL intensity at $490\ \mathrm{nm}$ of In-MOFEu rise sharply in $5\mathrm{min}$ , before a slow increase for the remaining $30\mathrm{min}$ (as shown in inset of Fig. 3b). The above results indicate that $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ -induced fluorescence enhancement reaction is fast. When different quality of In-MOF-Eu $\left(2{-}6\thinspace\mathrm{mg}\right)$ ) is immersed into chloroform $3\mathrm{mL}$ ) and the luminescence has been recorded at $_\mathrm{ex}=360\ \mathrm{nm}$ in Fig. S10a (supporting information), in which we can see the intensity of ligand-central emission rises gradually with the increase of In-MOF-Eu. In the Fig. S10b (supporting information), it is obvious that the histogram of ratios of I $(490\ \mathrm{nm})/\mathrm{\mathrm{~I~}}(615\ \mathrm{nm})$ and the inserted line chart with ratio values show that the ratio will reach a constant value at last, in spite of the quality of In-MOF-Eu is increased continually.
The regenerable performance has also been investigated to evaluate the feasibility of the luminescent probe, which plays an important role in its sensing applications. We attempted to disperse In-MOF-Eu into the solution of $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ for 10 minutes, and then the sample is centrifuged, ultrasonically washed with ethanol for three times. After the intensity of $\mathrm{I}_{490}/\mathrm{I}_{615}$ reduction induced by $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ , the following ultrasonic washing would make the ratio intensity recovers to the level before $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ addition, as shown in Fig. S11 (supporting information). Five runs performed by sequential addition of $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ and ultrasonic washing, the luminescent intensity of the recycled In-MOF-Eu are well consistent with that of the original one, suggesting that In-MOF-Eu could be reused to detect $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ . In order to study the mechanism of fluorescence enhancement of In-MOF-Eu in $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ , some possible explanation has been given. Only the ligand-central emission becomes stronger, indicating that chloroform has only effect on ligand. The strong electron-withdrawing property of three chlorine in chloroform make the H with some positive charge, while the pyridine ring is an electron rich group and its N has lone pair electrons, which can provide electrons with H to form a kind of hydrogen bond, [55- 57] as shown in Fig. S12a. The existence of hydrogen bond can also be proved in Fig. S12b, because the positions of the peaks red-shift from $297{\mathrm{nm}}$ to $313~\mathrm{nm}$ . The effects of luminescent enhancement of MOF caused by hydrogen-bonding interaction, the $\mathbf{N}$ in the structure of MOF as a hydrogen-bonding donator can decrease electron deficiency by bonding to hydrogen-bonding-accepting analytes.[58,59]
## 3.4. Detection of anions
Simultaneously, the research of anion-sensing function is conducted by immersing In-MOF-Eu in various anion aqueous solutions (anion $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathbf{\Psi}}&{{}=\mathbf{\Psi}\mathbf{F}^{-}}\end{array}$ , $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ , $\mathbf{Br}^{-}$ , I−, ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-}$ , MnO4-, $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}.$ ). The luminescence intensity of the In-MOF-Eu dispersed into different aqueous solution of various anions $(10^{-2}~\mathbf{M})$ $(3~\mathrm{mg}~/~3~\mathrm{mL})$ (excited monitored at $360\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) is presented in Fig. 4, indicating that the difference of anions has a great influence in the luminescence intensity of In-MOF-Eu. Compared with the above result, the effect of anions on ligand-central emission is concert with characteristic $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ emission. A striking phenomenon can be mentioned is that $\overline{{\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}}}$ has the distinct quenching effect on the luminescent emission and leads to a dark emission, but luminescent emission of the rest can be observed easily. It should be noted that, based on the concentration of these anions, luminescence intensity of the In-MOF-Eu in $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}$ has reduced, but there is a clear discrimination with $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ . Also, naked eyes can distinguish the dark solution of them from the bright red one of $\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}$ according to the inset of Fig. 4a. Histogram of Fig. 4b manifests that luminescence intensity of the $\mathrm{^5D_{0}}-\mathrm{^7F}_{2}$ of In-MOFEu dispersed into different aqueous solution of $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ can be ignored virtually. As can be seen from Fig. S13 (supporting information), PXRD patterns point out that the structure of In-MOF-Eu remains completed after immersing into those different anions.
Furthermore, we evaluate the quenching effects of anions on the luminescence of In-MOF-Eu via the fluorescence decay time of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ , as shown in Table. S3. Most of the anions have little effects on the luminescence lifetime of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ , whereas $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ anions exhibit significant reduction in the luminescence lifetimes which displays undetectable. Subsequently, $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ is chosen for further study of quenching effects. The influence of other anions (F−, Cl−, $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ , I−, ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}^{-}$ , ${\mathrm{SO}}_{4}^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-}$ and $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}$ ) on the detection of $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ is also determined. In accordance with Fig. 4a, there are only slight changes in the emission intensity of In-MOF-Eu in the presence of other anions. Upon the introduction of $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ to the mixture of In-MOF-Eu and other anions, the fluorescence is significantly quenched (Fig. S14a-b, supporting information). This observation suggests the interference from conventional anions can be neglected, further convincing the high selectivity of In-MOF-Eu for $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ detection and the potential of In-MOFEu for practical use. For better understanding the fluorescence quenching by concentration-dependent studies in the luminescence properties of In-MOF-Eu when $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ are present are carried out. As demonstrated in Fig. 5a, the emission intensity of the In-MOF-Eu suspension declines sharply with the increase of $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ concentration from 0 to $500~{\upmu\mathbf{M}}$ . Quantitatively, this quenching effect can be rationalized by the Stern–Volmer equation:
$$
\mathrm{I_{\mathrm{0}}/I=1+K_{\mathrm{sv}}\times\mathrm{[M]}}
$$
Where $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{sv}}$ is the quenching constant, [M] is the $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ concentration and the values $\mathbf{I}_{0}$ and I are the luminescence intensity of the In-MOF-Eu suspension without and with addition of $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ , respectively (Fig. 5b). The linear correlation coefficient (R) in the $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{sv}}$ curse of In-MOF-Eu with $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ is 0.9919, which suggests that the quenching effect of $\mathbf{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ on the luminescence of In-MOF-Eu fits the SternVolmer model well. The $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{sv}}$ value is calculated as $2.07\times10^{5}$ . From the slope of the fitting line (K) and the standard deviation for fluorescence measurements of blank solutions $(\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{b}})$ , the detection limit $(3\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{b}}\mathrm{~/~}$ K) of In-MOF-Eu towards $\mathbf{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ is calculated to be $1.47{\times}10^{-4}\upmu$ , which can be seen in the Calc. S1 of support information.
In order to further determine whether the $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ will influence on the $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ in real sample determination. We also measure the luminescence intensity of the In-MOF-Eu that dispersed into aqueous solution of $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ and $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ with different concentration $\mathrm{0-1.0\mmol/L)}$ at $\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}=360~\mathrm{nm}$ , as shown in Fig. S15 (ESI). It is found that when the concentration is $0.5\mathrm{mmol/L}$ or less, the contrast among these intensity of these solutions is relatively large $>7.14$ folds), indicating that when the concentration of the object is less than $0.5\ \mathrm{mmol}/\mathrm{L}$ , $\mathrm{{\bfMnO}_{4}}^{-}$ can be easier identified. Therefore, in contrast to $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}{}^{2\cdot}$ -, $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ has a stronger quenching effect of In-MOF-Eu. This above discussion has been given in manuscript. So, in the real sample determination, theoretically, when the concentration of $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ is less than $0.5\mathrm{mmol/L}$ , the influence of $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ can be ignored.
It should be noted here that the quenching effects on luminescence of MOFs by ions originates are very complicated and still not fully understood. To the date, there are several approaches: (1) the interaction between ions and organic ligands; (2) the collapse of the crystal structure; (3) the competitive UV absorption. [23, 60-61] It is well established that the emission intensity of lanthanide ions depends on the efficiency of energy transfer from the ligand to $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ to a certain degree. UV–vis spectra are used to investigate the mechanisms of the quenching effect. As can be clearly seen from the Fig. S16 (supporting information), the absorption peak of ligands within In-MOF-Eu is almost mantled by the wide absorption of $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ . The result argues that the quench effect is caused by the competition of $\mathbf{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ with the organic ligand for absorption of light energy, finally reducing the efficiency of energy transfer from the ligand to the lanthanide ions.
For the rapid detection of anions, the filter paper is immersed in aqueous solutions with different concentrations of $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ for 1 min and then exposed to air for drying for preparing fluorescence test paper. Optical images of Fig. 6 describe that, under the irradiation of UV light of $254\ \mathrm{nm}$ , the fluorescent colors of the test paper changed from bright red to dark red, faint red and finally black with increase of $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ (10 µM, 25 µM, $50~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , $100~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , $250~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ and $500~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ . Considering the results above, naked eyes can distinguish the colors of different intensities, thus making the detection simple and portable.
To test the applicability of the developed ratiometric and colorimetric PL sensor for $\mathbf{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ detection, the real samples, explored by two different pond water samples, are analyzed regularly. Then, the samples are spiked with standard solutions containing different concentrations of $\mathrm{{MnO}_{4}}^{-}$ . The results are summarized in Table S4, which indicate that the as-prepared In-MOF-Eu can be practically employed to detect $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ in pond samples.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, In-MOF (In(OH)bpydc) has been prepared and chosen as a parent supporter to encapsulate $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ cations, which are used to design as a stable fluorescence sensor for organic small molecules and anions. Studying of the luminescence properties reveals that In-MOF-Eu shows high selectivity for sensing chloroform among different small molecules, whose color can be distinctly improved via the enhanced ligand-central emission, whereas the characteristic emission of $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}$ stands still. In-MOF-Eu also can be employed as luminescent probe for sensing $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ in aqueous solution. Moreover, the quenching effect of $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ on In-MOF-Eu luminescence is not influenced by the presence of other conventional anions. Further study shows $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ has a good detection limit of 62.84 $\upmu\mathbf{M}$ and a broad linear range $(0.1-500~\mathrm{mM})$ . Fluorescence test paper is prepared for easier sensing, the colors will be observed changing from red to black or blue by naked eyes under the irradiation of UV light when those are immersed in different intensities of $\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ . Further study and speculation of the mechanism of the quenching effect have been investigated by UV–vis spectra. It is believable that this sensor has numerous desirable properties and is a cost-effective sensing material.
